        <!-- SECTION 1: Why a Surface Cleaner Beats a Spray Wand -->
        <div id="why-surface-cleaner" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why a Surface Cleaner Attachment Beats a Standard Spray Wand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Anyone who has spent an afternoon cleaning a driveway with just a spray wand knows the frustration: no matter how careful you are with your overlapping passes, the finished surface almost always shows faint stripes once it dries. A <strong>surface cleaner attachment</strong> eliminates that problem by design. Instead of a single narrow jet moving in a straight line, a surface cleaner uses two or four nozzles mounted on a bar that spins rapidly beneath an enclosed housing, sweeping the same strip of concrete dozens of times per second from multiple angles. The result is genuinely even, streak-free cleaning that a hand-held wand simply cannot replicate, no matter how skilled the operator.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed is the second major advantage. Because the spinning nozzles cover a full 15-to-20-inch swath with each pass instead of the few inches a wand jet covers, a surface cleaner can clean a two-car driveway in a fraction of the time a wand would take, often <strong>cutting total cleaning time by half or more</strong>. That matters enormously if you're cleaning a large area before sealing it, since sealer manufacturers require a clean, dry surface and every extra hour spent scrubbing with a wand is an hour the sealing project gets delayed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third advantage is one most first-time buyers don't anticipate: <strong>water containment</strong>. A standard wand sprays water in an open jet that bounces unpredictably off the surface, soaking siding, windows, parked cars, and anyone standing nearby. A surface cleaner's enclosed housing and splash skirt trap the vast majority of that spray underneath the deck, directing dirty runoff downward and outward at ground level instead of misting it across your garage door or your neighbor's fence. This containment is especially valuable when cleaning close to a house foundation, a wood-sided garage, or delicate landscaping.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
