        <!-- SECTION 2: Types of Surface Cleaners -->
        <div id="types-of-surface-cleaners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Surface Cleaners: A Buying Guide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every surface cleaner attachment is built the same, and matching the right type to your pressure washer and your job size is the single biggest factor in getting even, streak-free results. Here's what actually differs between models, and why it matters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Diameter: Matching the Deck to Your PSI and GPM</h3>
          <p>
            Surface cleaners typically come in 12-to-20-inch diameters, and larger decks require more water flow (GPM) to spin the nozzle bar at an effective speed. A 20-inch quad-nozzle deck paired with a low-GPM electric washer will spin sluggishly and clean unevenly, while a compact 15-inch dual-nozzle deck can spin nicely on either a gas or electric unit. As a rule of thumb, match a 15-16 inch deck to washers producing 2.0-3.0 GPM, and reserve 18-20 inch decks for gas washers producing 3.5 GPM or more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas vs. Electric Compatibility</h3>
          <p>
            Gas pressure washers typically deliver 2800-4000 PSI and 2.5-4.0 GPM, which is enough flow to spin even a large quad-nozzle bar quickly and evenly. Electric pressure washers usually top out around 1500-2000 PSI and lower GPM, so an attachment rated for gas-level output will often spin too slowly on an electric unit, producing the exact semicircle streaking a surface cleaner is supposed to eliminate. Buyers with electric washers should look specifically for attachments marketed as electric-compatible, like the <strong>Karcher T-Racer T400</strong>, rather than assuming any surface cleaner will work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dual-Nozzle vs. Quad-Nozzle Designs</h3>
          <p>
            Dual-nozzle bars use two opposing jets and are the traditional, most common design — simple, reliable, and easy to maintain. Quad-nozzle bars use four jets spaced around the bar, which lets the bar spin faster at the same GPM and generally produces a more even finish on textured or stamped concrete. The tradeoff is that quad-nozzle bars split the available water flow four ways instead of two, so they need a higher-GPM washer to spin properly in the first place — a quad-nozzle attachment on a low-flow electric washer often underperforms a well-matched dual-nozzle unit.
          </p>

