        <!-- SECTION 5: How to Use a Surface Cleaner -->
        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Use a Surface Cleaner: The Complete Guide
          </h2>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner attachment is genuinely easy to use once you understand the basics, but a few small technique mistakes are responsible for almost every disappointing result we've seen homeowners report.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Connect the Attachment to Your Wand</h3>
          <p>
            Remove your standard spray wand tip and attach the surface cleaner's quick-connect fitting directly to the pressure washer wand or hose, depending on the model. Most attachments use a universal 1/4-inch quick-connect that simply clicks into place; brand-specific fittings like Karcher's bayonet connection require either a matched washer or a separate universal adapter. Double-check the fitting is fully seated and locked before turning on the water — a partially connected fitting can blow off under pressure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Set Your Pressure and Test the Spin</h3>
          <p>
            With the water running, lower the attachment onto the surface and confirm the nozzle bar spins freely and at a consistent speed. If it spins slowly, chugs, or stalls, your pressure washer likely isn't supplying enough GPM for that attachment's nozzle configuration — this is most common when a quad-nozzle bar is paired with an electric washer. If the bar spins evenly, you're ready to start cleaning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Technique and Overlap Pattern</h3>
          <p>
            Push the cleaner in slow, straight, overlapping passes at a steady walking pace — moving too quickly is the single most common cause of streaking, since the nozzles simply don't have enough dwell time to lift every bit of grime. Overlap each pass by <strong>two to three inches</strong> with the previous one, and keep the deck flat against the surface at all times; tilting the housing even slightly reduces suction and lets overspray and dirty water escape from underneath. Work in straight, parallel rows rather than random or circular patterns, similar to mowing a lawn, to ensure complete, even coverage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Work the Edges Separately</h3>
          <p>
            Surface cleaners are excellent at open, flat areas but can't get flush against walls, fence lines, or garage door tracks because of the housing's diameter. Finish edges and corners with your standard spray wand tip after the main surface cleaning pass, or run the surface cleaner as close as possible and touch up the remaining strip by hand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Clean the Caster Wheels After Every Use</h3>
          <p>
            The casters are the most common failure point on any surface cleaner, since grit and dirt work into the wheel bearings during use and cause them to bind or wear unevenly over time. After each job, rinse the underside of the deck and the casters thoroughly with clean water, and periodically remove the wheels entirely to clear packed debris from the axles. A quick rinse takes under a minute and meaningfully extends the life of the casters, especially on models with plastic or open bushings rather than sealed bearings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Choosing the Right Size -->
        <div id="choosing-the-right-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Choosing the Right Size for Your Driveway, Patio, and Pressure Washer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Getting size right matters more with surface cleaners than almost any other pressure washer accessory, because an undersized deck on a huge driveway wastes hours, while an oversized deck on a small patio is clumsy to maneuver and can overwhelm a modest pressure washer's flow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match Diameter to Square Footage</h3>
          <p>
            For patios and walkways under 300 square feet, a compact 12-15 inch deck like the Briggs & Stratton or Karcher T-Racer T400 is easiest to maneuver around furniture, planters, and tight corners. For a standard two-car driveway in the 400-800 square foot range, a 15-16 inch dual-nozzle deck like the Stinger Whirl-a-Way hits the sweet spot between coverage and control. For driveways, aprons, or commercial lots over 800 square feet, stepping up to an 18-20 inch quad-nozzle deck like the Generac or Mi-T-M meaningfully reduces the total number of passes and the time spent on the job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match Diameter to Your Pressure Washer's Rating</h3>
          <p>
            Before buying based on square footage alone, confirm your pressure washer can actually supply enough PSI and GPM to spin the larger deck effectively. A general guideline: 12-15 inch decks work well from roughly 1500 PSI / 1.5 GPM upward, covering most electric washers. 15-16 inch decks want at least 2800 PSI / 2.3 GPM, typically a mid-range gas washer. 17-20 inch decks need 3200 PSI / 3.0 GPM or more, and quad-nozzle designs at that size really want 3.5-4.0 GPM to spin properly. Buying a deck larger than your washer can support is the most common surface cleaner mistake — the bar will spin sluggishly and leave the same streaking problem the attachment was supposed to eliminate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Size Up Anyway</h3>
          <p>
            If you frequently clean large areas and your pressure washer can support it, sizing up to an 18-20 inch quad-nozzle deck pays for itself quickly in time saved, even if your current job is only moderately large. Conversely, if you only clean a small patio a few times a year, there's little benefit to an oversized deck that's harder to store and maneuver around furniture and landscaping.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size surface cleaner do I need for my driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical single-car driveway or small patio (under 400 square feet), a compact 15-inch surface cleaner like the Briggs & Stratton or Ryobi Universal is sufficient. For a standard two-car driveway (400-800 square feet), a 16-inch dual-nozzle unit like the Stinger Whirl-a-Way is the sweet spot of coverage and maneuverability. For large driveways, aprons, or commercial lots over 800 square feet, an 18-20 inch quad-nozzle model like the Generac or Mi-T-M dramatically cuts total cleaning time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a surface cleaner on a patio or deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Surface cleaners work extremely well on concrete and paver patios, and on composite decking, but require caution on wood decking. On wood, drop the pressure well below what you'd use on concrete, keep the cleaner moving continuously, and never let it linger in one spot, since a spinning bar can gouge or fuzz softwood boards. Composite and paver patios handle full pressure without issue and are actually easier to clean evenly with a surface cleaner than with a standard wand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What PSI do I need for a surface cleaner attachment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most residential surface cleaners are rated between 3000 and 3700 PSI for gas-powered pressure washers, and between 1500 and 2000 PSI for electric units. What matters more than PSI alone is matching the attachment's rated GPM to your washer's actual output, since GPM drives how fast the nozzle bar spins. A mismatch, such as a high-PSI attachment paired with a low-GPM electric washer, produces uneven, streaky results even if the pressure rating looks compatible on paper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do surface cleaners fit any pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most surface cleaners use a standard 1/4-inch quick-connect fitting, which fits the vast majority of consumer gas and electric pressure washers. Some brands, notably Karcher, use a proprietary bayonet-style connection that requires either a brand-matched attachment or an inexpensive universal adapter. Always check your pressure washer's wand fitting before buying, and look for attachments that explicitly advertise universal compatibility or include an adapter kit if you're unsure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does it matter if my pressure washer is gas or electric?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Gas pressure washers typically produce 2800-4000 PSI and 2.5-4.0 GPM, giving surface cleaners enough flow to spin the nozzle bar quickly for even coverage. Electric pressure washers usually top out around 1500-2000 PSI and lower GPM, which can cause a gas-rated surface cleaner to spin too slowly and clean unevenly. For electric units, choose an attachment specifically rated for electric-level PSI and GPM, such as the Karcher T-Racer T400, rather than a general gas-rated model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop my surface cleaner from streaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Streaking is almost always caused by moving the cleaner too quickly, holding it unevenly so one side lifts off the surface, or using an attachment that's mismatched to your washer's PSI and GPM output. Move the cleaner at a slow, steady walking pace, keep the deck flat against the surface at all times, and overlap each pass by two to three inches. If streaking persists after correcting technique, the nozzle bar may be worn or the pressure washer may not be supplying enough flow to spin it evenly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a surface cleaner damage concrete or pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A properly used surface cleaner is actually gentler on most hard surfaces than a standard spray wand, because the enclosed spinning nozzles distribute pressure evenly across a wider area instead of concentrating it in one spot. That said, keeping the cleaner in one place too long, using excessive pressure on soft or aging pavers, or running a high-PSI gas-rated cleaner on decorative stamped or colored concrete can etch the surface. Test an inconspicuous area first and keep the cleaner moving continuously.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I maintain a surface cleaner attachment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                After each use, rinse the underside of the deck and the casters to remove grit, which otherwise works into the wheel bearings and causes them to bind. Periodically remove the caster wheels and clean debris from the axles, check the nozzles for clogs or wear by inspecting the spray pattern, and store the attachment somewhere dry to prevent the brass or steel quick-connect fitting from corroding. A quick rinse after every job adds years to the casters and nozzle bar.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Surface Cleaner Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner attachment is the difference between striping a driveway and cleaning it evenly, provided it is matched to the machine and used at a steady pace.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mismatching the Cleaner to the Washer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Surface cleaners are rated for a flow and pressure range. Run one too large for the machine and the bar barely spins, leaving arcs of dirt; run one rated well below the washer's output and it can damage the swivel. Match the GPM and PSI rating rather than the diameter alone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Moving Too Fast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The spinning bar needs time over each patch of surface, and a quick pass leaves a faint spiral pattern that only becomes obvious once the concrete dries. Move at a slow, steady walking pace with each pass overlapping the last by several inches.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using It on Soft or Damaged Surfaces</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Surface cleaners concentrate a lot of energy into a small area. On old, low-strength concrete, soft wood decking, or loose pavers, they strip cement paste, fuzz the grain, and blow out joint sand. Test in a corner before committing to the whole area.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Detergent for Oil and Organic Staining</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A surface cleaner removes what water and pressure can remove. Oil in the pores and mildew rooted in the surface need chemistry — a degreaser or an oxygenated cleaner applied and given dwell time first. Water alone lightens those stains and leaves them behind.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

